{"data":{"status":true,"competition":{"id":1766924,"visibility":"public","theme_colour":null,"paid":0,"title":"Worst Game Wins - Game Jam","festival_id":null,"organization_id":1161719,"web_url":null,"start_date":"2026-10-06T00:00:00+05:30","end_date":"2026-10-10T00:00:00+05:30","location":null,"type":"hackathons","public_url":"hackathons\/worst-game-wins-game-jam-indian-institute-of-information-technology-kottayam-1766924","region":"online","details":"<p><strong>The Worst Game Wins | 2-Day Online Game Jam<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Make the worst game possible. Win by being the best at it.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Most game jams reward polish, balance, and great design. This one rewards the opposite, with one catch: your terrible game must still be a complete, playable, fully committed piece of work.<\/p>\n<p><strong>About the Event<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>The Worst Game Wins<\/strong> is a 2-day online game jam where teams intentionally build the <em>worst possible game<\/em> around a given theme. Think clunky mechanics, absurd controls, nonsensical rules, and gloriously awful UX, all executed with total commitment to the bit.<\/p>\n<p>This is <strong>not<\/strong> a \"do nothing\" event. Bad design is the goal, but low effort is not. Judges are looking for teams who put real craft into making something deliberately terrible.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The Challenge<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>On Day 1, teams receive a <strong>theme<\/strong>&nbsp;that their game must clearly implement. From there, you have two days to design, build, test, and polish your masterpiece of awfulness, then submit it for judging.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Minimum Requirements<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>To qualify, every submission must:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Be playable start to finish<\/strong>: no crashes, no placeholder or empty builds<\/li>\n<li><strong>Clearly implement the theme<\/strong>: the theme must be identifiable in the game<\/li>\n<li><strong>Include at least one core mechanic<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Have a win\/lose condition<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Have basic UI<\/strong>: a menu and\/or instructions screen<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\ufe0f <strong>\"Bad\" must be deliberate.<\/strong> Missing features or broken builds are not a substitute for bad design. Intentional badness is the whole point, and it will be judged.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>Judging Criteria<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Entries will be evaluated on:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hilarity<\/strong>: Does it make people laugh (or rage)?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Creativity<\/strong>: How original and inventive is the badness?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Intentional Bad Design<\/strong>: Is the awfulness clearly deliberate and well executed?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Thematic Relevance<\/strong>: How well does the game reflect the given theme?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Commitment to the Bit<\/strong>: How fully did the team go all in on the \"worst game\" concept, while still delivering a complete, playable game?<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Prizes<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Total prize pool: \u20b92,500<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Prize distribution among the top teams will be announced by the organizers.<\/p>\n<hr \/>\n<h2>\ufe0f Event Details<\/h2>\n<table style=\"width: 99.8425%; height: 122px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38.022%; height: 21px;\"><strong>Format<\/strong><\/td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.4544%; height: 21px;\">Online<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38.022%; height: 21px;\"><strong>Duration<\/strong><\/td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.4544%; height: 21px;\">2 Days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38.022%; height: 21px;\"><strong>Dates<\/strong><\/td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.4544%; height: 21px;\">October 10&ndash;11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38.022%; height: 21px;\"><strong>Team Size<\/strong><\/td>\n<td style=\"width: 58.4544%; height: 21px;\"><em>1-4 members<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<hr \/>\n<p><strong>Who Should Participate?<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Game developers, designers, artists, and writers<\/li>\n<li>Beginners looking for a low-pressure way to build their first game<\/li>\n<li>Experienced devs who want to flex their creativity (and sense of humor)<\/li>\n<li>Anyone who has ever said, \"I could make a worse game than this\"<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>You can use any game engine or tool (Unity, Godot, Unreal, GameMaker, Scratch, web-based, etc.).<\/p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Submission Guidelines<\/strong><\/p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Submit a single <strong>.zip file<\/strong> containing:<\/p>\n<ul dir=\"ltr\">\n<li><strong>Your complete game<\/strong>: a playable build that runs from start to finish without crashes (include all files needed to run it, plus brief run instructions if the setup isn't obvious)<\/li>\n<li><strong>A README file<\/strong> (<code dir=\"ltr\">README.txt<\/code> or <code dir=\"ltr\">README.md<\/code>) with a short description of your game, including:\n<ul dir=\"ltr\">\n<li>The name of your game and your team<\/li>\n<li>A brief description of what the game is<\/li>\n<li>How your game implements the theme<\/li>\n<li>Which \"bad\" design choices you made on purpose (so judges can appreciate the craft)<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Make sure the zip opens cleanly and the game runs as described. Submit before the deadline at the end of Day 2.<\/p>","overall_prizes":null,"regn_open":1,"regn_type":1,"regn_url":null,"regn_email":"","registration_clicks":0,"user_id":21143511,"moderation_status":1,"overall_leaderboard":0,"reject_reason":null,"display_date":null,"created_at":"2026-10-06T23:06:58+05:30","updated_at":"2026-10-06T23:42:13+05:30","communication_sent":null,"certificate_at":null,"remarks":null,"branding":0,"disable_auto_rounds":null,"certificate_config":null,"subtype":"online_coding_challenge","combined_leaderboard":null,"source":null,"short_id":"R8Q0CnN","hashtags":null,"participation_certificate":0,"winne